# =========================
# Project-specific
# =========================
# NOTE ON GLOBS: do NOT add bare `*.bin`, `*.csv`, or `*.log` rules here.
# Earlier revisions did, and each one silently un-tracked a small REQUIRED
# file: `*.csv` dropped constants/atom_types.csv (listed in the params
# registry's layout.required, so a fresh clone could not build a force field),
# `*.bin` dropped every potential table, and the upstream template's `*.log`
# (line 59, from its Django section) dropped
# examples/actin/legacy/outputs/acta_T_0.600.log -- the cited provenance
# source for the legacy H-bond parity value. Scope output rules to the
# directories that actually produce output instead, and keep the negations
# below. scikit-build-core filters WHEEL contents through .gitignore
# (build/_pathutil.py -> each_unignored_file), so an over-broad rule here
# also silently drops files from the built wheel.

# -- Editor / agent state ------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.cursor/
.claude/

# -- Build trees / CMake artifacts ---------------------------------------
/build*/
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
cmake_install.cmake
_deps/
_skbuild/
.cmake/
compile_commands.json

# -- Large generated parameter payloads ----------------------------------
# The fitted .bin tables are large and regenerated offline;
# the shipped, re-encoded parameter set under pymcpu/data/params is NOT.
# GLOB the directory name, not the literal `mcpu_params`: each force field's
# parameter set gets its OWN relpath (mcpu26_params/, ...), and a rule keyed on
# one fit's spelling silently fails to ignore the next one's tables -- which
# scikit-build-core would then ship in the sdist.
src/pymcpu/parameters/pretrained/*/*_params/
src/pymcpu/parameters/pretrained/*/protein_cache/
resources/

# -- Simulation outputs --------------------------------------------------
/checkpoints/
/seg_states/
/out_*/
/output/
/logs/
/tmp_*/
examples/frames/
examples/actin/output/
examples/actin/bench_result.json
log.txt
coords.txt
*.xtc
*.dcd
*.chk
*.npz
*.h5
*.hdf5

# -- Simulation CSV output (scoped: atom_types.csv must stay tracked) ----
/*.csv
examples/**/*.csv
tests/**/*.csv

# -- Profiling -----------------------------------------------------------
*callgrind*
profile.json
perfomance.txt
perf_reports/

# -- Cluster job output --------------------------------------------------
*.out
*.err

# -- User inputs (only the template is tracked) --------------------------
/inputs/*
!/inputs/template.yaml
!/inputs/README*

# -- Legacy MCPU reference inputs (NOT distributed) ----------------------
# Inputs to a C binary that is not in this repository and is not rebuildable
# here. Ignored rather than absent-by-accident: copy the tree in to run
# tests/legacy_parity/test_hbond_ablation_ladder.py locally and it will not
# end up committed. See docs/hbond_legacy_parity.md.
# NOTE the `/**`, not a bare trailing slash. Excluding the DIRECTORY stops
# git descending into it at all, and a later `!` on a file inside then has
# no effect -- the negation silently does nothing. Excluding the contents
# instead leaves the directory traversable so the negation below works.
examples/actin/legacy/**
# ...except this one. 2 KB, and it is the legacy reference that
# tests/physics/forcefield/test_chi_topology.py and
# test_chi_moved_atoms_topology.py validate pyMCPU's own chi-atom topology
# JSON against. Those are live tests of pyMCPU behaviour, not legacy
# re-derivation, so the file earns its place.
!examples/actin/legacy/config_files/
!examples/actin/legacy/config_files/amino_torsion.data

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KORP energy maps -- NOT distributed
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 316 MiB, well over PyPI's per-file limit, so pyMCPU requires it to be
# supplied rather than shipping it. See docs/physics_notes/korp_6d.md.
# Deliberately ABOVE the MUST SHIP block below: git honors the last matching
# pattern, and a rule placed after it is silently ineffective.
korp6Dv1.bin
korp_loop_v*.bin
Korp6Dv1*.txz
*.korp.bin

# =========================================================================
# MUST SHIP -- keep this block LAST
# =========================================================================
# git honors the LAST matching pattern, so these un-ignore rules have to come
# after every broad rule above. Putting them next to the section they relate
# to does NOT work: `!pymcpu/data/params/**` placed earlier was silently
# overridden by the later `*.npz` rule, which would have shipped a wheel with
# no parameter tables in it. `tests/config/test_shipped_params_are_trackable.py`
# pins this.
!pymcpu/data/params/**
!src/pymcpu/parameters/pretrained/*/constants/*.csv
